{"indexedPages":5,"totalPages":5,"version":"461","text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON CIRCUITS AND SYSTEMS—II: EXPRESS BRIEFS, VOL. 68, NO. 8, AUGUST 2021 3017\nInteracting Multiple Model Based on Maximum\nCorrentropy Kalman Filter\nXuxiang Fan , Gang Wang , Jiachen Han , and Yinghui Wang\nAbstract—Traditional interacting multiple model Kalman filter (IMM-KF) was derived for linear discrete time system with Markovian coefficients and it works well under Gaussian noise. In practice, when the system is affected by the impulsive noise, the performance of the traditional IMM-KF model becomes poor. Then, maximum correntropy Kalman filter (MCKF) which has advantages in dealing with non-Gaussian noise was proposed. However, the performance of traditional MCKF becomes poor when the motion state is not single. In this brief, we propose a new algorithm called interacting multiple model based on maximum correntropy Kalman filter (IMM-MCKF) by combining interacting multiple model (IMM) with MCKF to deal with the impulsive noise. Also, the traditional MCKF works poorly when the kernel bandwidth is small, we made a further improvement by changing the kernel. Simulation examples are given to demonstrate the effectiveness of the new algorithms.\nIndex Terms—Maximum correntropy Kalman filter, maximum correntropy criterion, interacting multiple model, mixed-kernel, root mean square error.\nI. INTRODUCTION\nT\nHE KALMAN filtering (KF) [1]–[3] is a least squares linear filter, and is widely used in target tracking [4], signal processing, system state estimation [5], navigation, control, and other fields. In recent years, as the motion models are becoming diverse, interacting multiple model Kalman filter (IMM-KF) [6]–[7] has been proposed in 1984 [8], which can work with more weighted fusion models effectively. Therefore, on the problem of maneuvering target processing, interacting multiple model (IMM) has the properties of higher precision and better stability. Now IMM-KF is widely used in maneuvering target tracking [6]–[9], positioning system [10], the navigation system [11], state estimation of the system [12], formation control [13], biomedical engineering domain [14]–[17], etc. IMM is an algorithm for automatic recognition and switching between models based on Bayesian theory [18]. Most of the IMM-KF algorithms are based on the minimum mean\nManuscript received December 24, 2020; revised February 10, 2021 and March 9, 2021; accepted March 19, 2021. Date of publication March 23, 2021; date of current version July 30, 2021. This brief was recommended by Associate Editor S. Hoyos. (Corresponding author: Gang Wang.)\nThe authors are with the School of Information and Communication Engineering, University of Electronic Science and Technology of China, Chengdu 611731, China (e-mail: 1794192658@qq.com; wanggang_hld@uestc.edu.cn; 591485561@qq.com; 460198266@qq.com). Color versions of one or more figures in this article are available at https://doi.org/10.1109/TCSII.2021.3068221. Digital Object Identifier 10.1109/TCSII.2021.3068221\nsquare error (MMSE) criterion which can effectively handling conventional Gaussian noise but may work poorly in the presence of non-Gaussian environments. In order to solve this problem, maximum correntropy criterion (MCC) [19]–[23] was proposed to apply in the KF, called maximum correntropy Kalman filter (MCKF) [24], which has advantages in dealing with non-Gaussian environment and higher accuracy in tracking and positioning process. However, the performance of traditional single model MCKF becomes poor when the motion state is not single. In addition, MCKF with a small kernel bandwidth will get better steady error normally [25] and [26]. However, we find the traditional MCKF cannot work when the kernel bandwidth is small. For real maneuvering target tracking, the motion state of the target is often not single, and we pursue a smaller steady error, so it’s necessary to propose a new algorithm to deal with these problems. In this brief, we combine the conventional IMM with MCKF to propose a new algorithm, which is called interacting multiple model based on maximum correntropy Kalman filter (IMM-MCKF). Then we change its Gaussian kernel to a linear combination of Gaussian kernel and Sigmoid kernel to get better steady error. At the same time, we analyze and compare the root mean square error (RMSE) of tracking data, and it is expected that IMM-MCKF will have better performance in the environment of non-Gaussian noise in the two-state motion model than the traditional IMM-KF algorithms and traditional MCKF.\nII. PRELIMINARIES\nIn traditional IMM-KF, suppose that there are n Kalman models. The process equation and measurement equation of model m are as follows (m = 1, 2, . . . , n) [8]:\n{ X(k + 1) = FmX(k) + wm(k),\nZ(k) = HmX(k) + rm(k), (1)\nwhere X(k) ∈ Rp represents the p-dimensional state vector at instant k, Z(k) ∈ Rq denotes the q-dimensional measurement vector at instant k, Fm is the state transition matrix of model m, and Hm is the observation transition matrix of model m. wm(k) and rm(k) are the state noise and observation noise with zero mean and covariance Qm and R. The Markov transition probability matrix is\nP=\n⎡\n⎢⎣\nP11 . . . P1n\n... . . . ...\nPn1 . . . Pnn\n⎤\n⎥⎦, (2)\n1549-7747 c© 2021 IEEE. Personal use is permitted, but republication/redistribution requires IEEE permission. See https://www.ieee.org/publications/rights/index.html for more information.\nAuthorized licensed use limited to: University of Electronic Science and Tech of China. Downloaded on October 15,2025 at 13:23:48 UTC from IEEE Xplore. Restrictions apply.\n\n\n 3018 IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON CIRCUITS AND SYSTEMS—II: EXPRESS BRIEFS, VOL. 68, NO. 8, AUGUST 2021\nwhere Pim is transition probability from model i to model m. In general, the classic IMM-KF algorithm model can be summarized into the following steps: (a) Input interaction: Calculate the probability of mixed normalized model from model i to model m:\nμim(k − 1|k − 1) = Pimμi(k − 1)/cm, (3)\nwhere cm =\nn∑\ni=1\nPimμi(k − 1), and μi(k − 1) is the model\nprobability. (b) Calculate the initial fusion state vector of model m:\nXˆ pre_m(k − 1|k − 1) =\n∑n\ni=1\nXˆ i(k − 1|k − 1)μim(k − 1|k − 1). (4)\n(c) Calculate the mixed covariance estimation of model m:\nPpre_m(k − 1|k − 1) =\nn ∑\ni=1\nμim(k − 1|k − 1)\n{\nPi(k − 1|k − 1) +\n[Xˆ i(k − 1|k − 1) − Xˆ pre_m(k − 1|k − 1)\n]\n[Xˆ i(k − 1|k − 1) − Xˆ pre_m(k − 1|k − 1)\n]T }\n. (5)\n(d) The KF is applied to model m:\nXˆ m(k|k) = Xˆ m(k|k − 1) + Km(k)υm(k), (6)\nwhere⎧⎪⎪⎨\n⎪⎪⎩\nXˆ m(k|k − 1) = FmXˆ pre_m(k − 1|k − 1), Pm(k|k − 1) = FmPpre_m(k − 1|k − 1)FT\nm + Qm,\nυm(k) = Z(k) − HmXˆ m(k|k − 1), Sm(k) = HmPm(k|k − 1)HT\nm + R,\n(7)\nKm(k) = Pm(k|k − 1)HT\nmS−1\nm (k). (8)\n(e) Observe the likelihood function of Z(k):\nm(k) = 1\n√2π |Sm(k)| exp\n(\n−1\n2υT\nm(k)S−1\nm (k)υm(k)\n)\n. (9)\n(f) Calculate the filtering covariance matrix:\nPm(k|k) = [I − Km(k)Hm]Pm(k|k − 1)\n= Pm(k|k − 1) − Km(k)Sm(k)KT\nm(k). (10)\n(g) Update the model probability:\nμm(k) = m(k)cm\nc , (11)\nwhere\nc=\nn ∑\nm=1\nm(k)cm. (12)\n(h) Output interaction: Calculate the fusion filter value:\nXˆ (k|k) =\n∑n\nm=1\nXˆ m(k|k)μm(k). (13)\n(i) Interact the covariance matrix of filtering error:\nP(k|k) =\nn ∑\nm=1\nμm(k){Pm(k|k)\n+ [Xˆ m(k|k) − Xˆ (k|k)][Xˆ m(k|k) − Xˆ (k|k)]T }. (14)\nIII. PROPOSED IMM-MCKF\nA. IMM Combined With MCKF\nIn the IMM-MCKF, we still assume that the observed target has n transfer equations, an interaction model with n inputs, and then calculate normalized mixed probability and covariance matrix. For the selected model m, Kalman filtering based on MCC is performed, then model probability is updated, and the interactive fusion filtering value is finally output. (m = 1, 2, . . . , n) Here, we refer to [8], [19] and give the new algorithm. Suppose the model is the same as IMM-KF we mentioned above, we summarize the basic steps of the IMM-MCKF as follows.\n1) Compute the Filter Value Corresponding to Each Model: (a)–(c), the same as those of IMM-KF (d) Compute the state predicted value and the state predicted error covariance:\nXˆ m(k|k − 1) = FmXˆ pre_m(k − 1|k − 1),\nPm(k|k − 1) = FmPpre_m(k − 1|k − 1)FT\nm + Qm. (15)\n(e) Calculate the new rate and residual error:\nυm(k) = Z(k) − HmXˆ m(k|k − 1),\nSm(k) = HmPm(k|k − 1)HT\nm + R. (16)\n(f) Preparation for calculating the filter value:\nDm(k) = B−1\nm (k)\n[ Xˆ m(k|k − 1) Z(k)\n] ,\nWm(k) = B−1\nm (k)\n[I\nHm(k)\n]\n, (17)\nwhere\nBm(k) =\n[ Bp_m(k|k − 1) 0 0 Br_m(k)\n]\n, (18)\nand Bp_m(k|k − 1), Br_m(k) are obtained by Cholesky decomposition of Pm(k|k − 1), R:\nPm(k|k − 1) = Bp_m(k|k − 1)BT\np_m(k|k − 1),\nR = Br_m(k)BT\nr_m(k). (19)\n(g) Let t = 1 and give initial value of the filter value for model m.\nXˆ m(k|k)0 = Xˆ m(k|k − 1), (20)\nwhere Xˆ m(k|k)t represents the estimated state of the iteration at t. (h) Calculate the filter value for mode m:\nXˆ m(k|k)t = Xˆ m(k|k − 1) + Km(k)υm(k), (21)\nwhere\nKm(k) = Pm(k|k − 1)HT\nm\n[\nHmPm(k|k − 1)HT\nm+Rm(k)\n]−1,\nPm(k|k − 1) = Bp_m(k|k − 1)φ−1\nx_m(k)BT\np_m(k|k − 1),\nRm(k) = Br_m(k)φ−1\ny_m(k)BT\nr_m(k),\nφx_m(k) = diag( Gσ\n[∇1_m(k)] · · · Gσ\n[∇p_m(k)] ),\nφy_m(k) = diag( Gσ\n[∇p+1_m(k)] · · · Gσ\n[∇p+q_m(k)] ), (22)\nAuthorized licensed use limited to: University of Electronic Science and Tech of China. Downloaded on October 15,2025 at 13:23:48 UTC from IEEE Xplore. Restrictions apply.\n\n\n FAN et al.: IMM BASED ON MCKF 3019\nwith\n∇m(k) = Dm(k) − Wm(k)Xˆ m(k|k)t−1\n= [ ∇1_m(k) · · · ∇p+q_m(k) ]T , (23)\nand Gaussian Kernel\nGσ [∇] = exp\n(\n− ∇2\n2σ 2\n)\n, (24)\nwhere σ is kernel bandwidth. (i) Calculate the lower limit value in the cycle:\nt_m =\n∣∣∣Xˆ m(k|k)t − Xˆ m(k|k)t−1\n∣∣∣\n∣∣∣Xˆ m(k|k)t−1\n∣∣∣\n. (25)\nIf t ≤ ε, set Xˆ m(k|k) = Xˆ m(k|k)t and continue to the next step. Else, let t = t + 1 and back to (h). (j) Calculate the posterior covariance matrix of the model m:\nPm(k|k) =\n[\nI − Km(k)Hm\n]\nPm(k|k − 1)\n[\nI − Km(k)Hm\n]T\n+ Km(k)RKT\nm(k). (26)\n(k) Update the probability of the model:\nμm(k) = m(k)cm\nc , (27)\nwhere\nc=\n∑n\nm=1\nm(k)cm,\nm(k) = 1\n√2π |Sm(k)| exp\n(\n−1\n2υT\nm(k)S−1\nm (k)υm(k)\n)\n, (28)\nwhere Sm(k) has been introduced in (16). 2) Interact the Filter Value:\nXˆ (k|k) =\nn ∑\nm=1\nXˆ m(k|k)μm(k), (29)\nP(k|k) =\nn ∑\nm=1\nμm(k)\n{\nPm(k|k)\n+\n[Xˆ m(k|k) − Xˆ (k|k)\n][Xˆ m(k|k) − Xˆ (k|k)\n]T }\n. (30)\nB. Computational Complexity\nIn this sub-section, we analyze the computational complexity of IMM-MCKF in terms of the floating operations and compare it with the traditional IMM-KF. Table I shows the computational complexities of some equations. The traditional IMM-KF mainly involves (3)-(8) and (26)-(30). We can conclude the computational complexity of IMM is\nSIMM = [8p3 + 9p2q + 7pq2 + (2n + 1)p2 − 2pq\n+ (4n − 1)p + 2q2 + q + n2 + 3n + 4]n\n− p2 − 2p − 1 + O(q3) + O(q2). (31)\nTABLE I COMPUTATIONAL COMPLEXITIES OF SOME EQUATIONS\nThe IMM-MCKF mainly involves (3)-(5), (15), (16) and (21)-(30). We can also get the computational complexity of IMM-MCKF is\nSIMM−MCKF = [(4L + 8)p3 + 4Lq3 + (4L + 7)p2q + (4L + 5)pq2\n+ (2n − 2L + 1)p2 − Lpq + (4n + 4L − 1)p\n+ (2 − 2L)q2 + (2L + 1)q + n2 + 3n + L + 4]n\n− p2 − 2p − 1 + LO(p3) + LO(q3) + O(q2). (32)\nThe average fixed-point iteration number L is relatively small [19], so the computational complexity of IMM-MCKF is slightly larger than the traditional IMM-KF.\nC. Kernel Change\nIn many cases, MCKF with a small kernel bandwidth performs better. However, we find the traditional MCKF cannot work when the kernel bandwidth is small. Here, we make a linear combination of Gaussian kernel and Sigmoid kernel to solve this problem. Therefore, a new algorithm can be proposed by changing Gaussian kernel (24) into mixed-kernel (33) based on IMMMCKF algorithm above. We called the new algorithm IMM based on improved MCKF (IMM-IMCKF). It can be expected that IMM-IMCKF has the smallest steady-state RMSEs in the following simulation compared with IMM-KF, MCKF and\nAuthorized licensed use limited to: University of Electronic Science and Tech of China. Downloaded on October 15,2025 at 13:23:48 UTC from IEEE Xplore. Restrictions apply.\n\n\n 3020 IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON CIRCUITS AND SYSTEMS—II: EXPRESS BRIEFS, VOL. 68, NO. 8, AUGUST 2021\nIMM-MCKF.\nL[x, y] = α exp\n(\n− ∇2\n2σ 2\n)\n+ (1 − α)tanh(β + χ), (33)\nwhere ∇ = x−y, = xy, 0 ≤ α ≤ 1 is the mixing coefficient, and β, χ are Sigmoid kernel parameters.\nIV. SIMULATIONS\nIn this part, we do a tracking simulation combining constant velocity (CV) with constant turning (CT) model, and compare the results under CV single model MCKF (MCKF-CV), traditional IMM-KF, IMM-MCKF and IMM-IMCKF. The target, whose initial location is (1000, 1000)/(m) and initial speed is (10, −10)/(m/s), first moves in a straight line, turns at a uniform angular speed, and then moves in a straight line. The simulation results are averaged over 500 Monte Carlo runs. Here, we refer to the simulation conditions in [9]. First of all, we consider the motion model of the two states, so here m=2, and the linear system is as follows:\n{ X(k + 1) = FmX(k) + wm(k),\nZ(k) = HmX(k) + rm(k), (34)\nwhere ω = π\n270 ,\nF1 =\n⎡\n⎢⎢⎣\n1100 0100 0011 0001\n⎤\n⎥⎥⎦, F2 =\n⎡\n⎢⎢⎢⎣\n1 sin(ω)\nω 0 cos(ω)−1\nω\n0 cos(ω) 0 − sin(ω) 0 1−cos(ω)\nω 1 sin(ω)\nω\n0 sin(ω) 0 cos(ω)\n⎤\n⎥⎥⎥⎦,\nH1 = H2 =\n[1 0 0 0 0010\n]\n, X(0) =\n⎡\n⎢⎢⎣\n1000 10 1000 −10\n⎤\n⎥⎥⎦, (35)\nand the Markov transition probability matrix is\nP=\n[ 0.99 0.01 0.01 0.99\n]\n. (36)\nSecondly, we consider that the noise in the measurement equation is impulse noise whose distribution is mixedGaussian [19]:\nr1(k) ∼ 0.9N(0, 0.012) + 0.1N(0, 2002),\nr2(k) ∼ 0.9N(0, 0.012) + 0.1N(0, 2002), (37)\nand the noise in the process equation is Gaussian:\nw1(k) ∼ G1N(0, Q1),\nw2(k) ∼ G2N(0, Q2), (38)\nwhere\nG1 =\n⎡\n⎢⎢⎣\n1\n20 10\n01\n2\n01\n⎤\n⎥⎥⎦, G2 =\n⎡\n⎢⎢⎣\n1\n20 10\n01\n2\n01\n⎤\n⎥⎥⎦, (39)\nQ1 =\n[ 0.12 0 0 0.12\n]\n, Q2 =\n[ 0.01442 0 0 0.01442\n]\n. (40)\nIn Figure 1, we set kernel bandwidth σ = 3 (it cannot be smaller or the algorithm will not work), and the loop restriction ε = 0.01 in MCKF-CV and IMM-MCKF, α = 0.9, σI = 1.1,\nFig. 1. Tracking results under four different filters.\nFig. 2. RMSEs of tracking under four different filters.\nTABLE II RMSES OF X-DIRECTION, Y-DIRECTION IN NON-GAUSSIAN NOISES UNDER FOUR DIFFERENT ALGORITHMS\nβ = 0.2, χ = 5in IMM-IMCKF and it shows the tracking results in non-Gaussian noises under these four algorithms. We can clearly see that MCKF-CV can’t work well when the motion state of target changes, IMM-KF, IMM-MCKF and IMM-IMCKF both doing better than MCKF-CV. Figure 2 shows RMSEs of x-direction, y-direction under IMM-KF, IMM-MCKF IMM-IMCKF and MCKF-CV in non-Gaussian noise as (37). IMM-KF converge foster than IMM-MCKF and IMM-IMCKF. But IMM-MCKF and IMMIMCKF have better steady-state error. Then we present in Table II specific numerical comparisons of the steady RMSEs in x-direction and y-direction under different algorithms. The numbers show that IMM-IMCKF is the best one compared with other three algorithms in this situation.\nAuthorized licensed use limited to: University of Electronic Science and Tech of China. Downloaded on October 15,2025 at 13:23:48 UTC from IEEE Xplore. Restrictions apply.\n\n\n FAN et al.: IMM BASED ON MCKF 3021\nTABLE III RMSES IN NON-GAUSSIAN NOISES WITH DIFFERENT KERNEL BANDWIDTH UNDER IMM-MCKF AND IMM-IMCKF\nTABLE IV RMSES IN GAUSSIAN NOISES UNDER FOUR FILTERS\nAnd the RMSEs of IMM-IMCKF are about 40 percent lower compared with IMM-KF. In Table III, we changed the kernel bandwidth and observed the RMSEs of IMM-MCKF, IMM-IMCKF in x-direction and y-direction. When σ gets smaller, the RMSEs become better. However, IMM-MCKF cannot work when σ is too small. At the same time, IMM-IMCKF still work well and get better results. Then we consider Gaussian noise in the simulation,\nr1(k) ∼ N(0, 202),\nr2(k) ∼ N(0, 202), (41)\nTable IV shows the performance of our proposed system under gaussian noise, where we set kernel bandwidth σ = 10, the loop restriction ε = 0.01. IMM-KF, IMM-MCKF, IMMIMCKF have almost identical steady RMSE. And they perform better than MCKF-CV.\nV. CONCLUSION\nA new IMM algorithm based on MCKF called IMMMCKF has been proposed. It has better tracking ability against impulsive noise by changing the filter gain of the original IMM-KF algorithm. Simulation results demonstrate that IMMMCKF can outperform the traditional IMM-KF and MCKF in the two-state motion model in the non-Gaussian noises, even though the computational complexity of IMM-MCKF is slightly larger than the traditional IMM-KF. Then further improvement has been made by changing kernel. And it is useful for IMM-MCKF, IMM-IMCKF to select an appropriate kernel bandwidth.\nREFERENCES\n[1] R. E. Kalman, “A new approach to linear filtering and prediction problems,” J. Basic Eng., vol. 82, no. 1, pp. 35–45, 1960. [2] T. Kailath, “An innovations approach to least-squares estimation—Part I: Linear filtering in additive white noise,” IEEE Trans. Autom. Control, vol. AC-13, no. 6, pp. 646–655, Dec. 1968.\n[3] Y. Huang, F. Zhu, G. Jia, and Y. Zhang, “A slide window variational adaptive Kalman filter,” IEEE Trans. Circuits Syst. II, Exp. Briefs, vol. 67, no. 12, pp. 3552–3556, Dec. 2020. [4] C. Lu, Y. Zhang, and Q. Ge, “Kalman filter based on multiple scaled multivariate skew normal variance mean mixture distributions with application to target tracking,” IEEE Trans. Circuits Syst. II, Exp. Briefs, vol. 68, no. 2, pp. 802–806, Feb. 2021. [5] Z. Wang, Y. Huang, Y. Zhang, G. Jia, and J. Chambers, “An improved Kalman filter with adaptive estimate of latency probability,” IEEE Trans. Circuits Syst. II, Exp. Briefs, vol. 67, no. 10, pp. 2259–2263, Oct. 2020. [6] G. Yao, R. Saltus, and A. Dani, “Image moment-based extended object tracking for complex motions,” IEEE Sensors J., vol. 20, no. 12, pp. 6560–6572, Jun. 2020. [7] W. Youn and H. M